  "articleBody": "When you're thinking about a senior home safety assessment, you want to make sure you're getting something really thorough and useful. First off, look for someone with specific experience in senior safety; they'll know exactly what to check. A good assessment should involve a detailed, room-by-room walk-through of your home. They should be checking for common hazards like loose rugs, inadequate lighting, and making sure grab bars are securely installed. Pay attention to whether they provide a written report with prioritized recommendations. You want clear, practical fixes, not just a list of problems. It’s also a huge plus if they understand aging-in-place modifications, so they can suggest changes that improve safety without making your home feel institutional. If you’re dealing with a home with multiple risk factors or you’re thinking about bigger renovations, finding a pro who can prioritize and design safer solutions is key.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}